\paragraph{Granularity ablation.}
The need for EDD---and its use of dedicated binary classifiers---is motivated by decision boundary dilution: as the number of classes increases, per-pair discriminability degrades.
Across the 9 atlas cells, mean accuracy drops from 99.5\% (binary: d0 vs.\ rest) to 78.3\% (4-class) to 62.9\% (6-class, Pythia nucleus d0--d5).
More critically, the d2\,vs.\,d3 pairwise AUC falls from 0.804 in the 4-class setting to 0.689 in the 6-class setting for Pythia nucleus---a 14\% relative decline attributable purely to boundary dilution, not loss of underlying signal.
This confirms that aggregate multi-class accuracy underestimates pairwise discriminability, justifying EDD's binary-pair design.

